WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.259 --> 00:00:07.259
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:12.039
Last year, an amazing neural network-based
technique appeared that was able to look at

00:00:12.039 --> 00:00:17.560
a bunch of unlabeled motion data, and learned
to weave them together to control the motion

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.330
of quadrupeds, like this wolf here.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:25.660
It was able to successfully address the shortcomings
of previous works, for instance, the weird

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:31.980
sliding motions have been eliminated, and
it was also capable of following some predefined

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.430
trajectories.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.739
This new paper continues research in this
direction by proposing a technique that is

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:43.969
also capable of interacting with its environment
or other characters, for instance, they can

00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:50.160
punch each other, and after the punch, they
can recover from undesirable positions, and

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.160
more.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:55.859
The problem formulation is as follows - it
is given the current state of the character

00:00:55.859 --> 00:01:01.179
and a goal, and you see here with blue how
it predicts the motion to continue.

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:06.150
It understands that we have to walk towards
the goal, that we are likely to fall when

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:12.250
hit by a ball, and it knows, that then, we
have to get up and continue our journey, and

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:14.710
eventually, reach our goal.

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:18.110
Some amazing life advice from the AI right
there.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:24.720
The goal here is also to learn something meaningful
from lots of barely labeled human motion data.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:29.510
Barely labeled means that a bunch of videos
are given almost as-is, without additional

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:33.530
information on what movements are being performed
in these videos.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:38.240
If we had labels for all this data that you
see here, it would say that this sequence

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.440
shows a jump, and these ones are for running.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.860
However, the labeling process takes a ton
of time and effort, so if we can get away

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:53.400
without it, that’s glorious, but, in return,
with this, we create an additional burden

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.940
that the learning algorithm has to shoulder.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:01.890
Unfortunately, the problem gets even worse
- as you see here, the number of frames contained

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.910
in the original dataset is very scarce.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:10.190
To alleviate this, the authors decided to
augment this dataset, which means trying to

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:16.870
combine parts of this data to squeeze out
as much information as possible.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:22.400
You see some examples here how this motion
data can be combined from many small segments,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.709
and in the paper, they show that the augmentation
helps us create even up to 10 to 30 times

00:02:28.709 --> 00:02:31.739
more training data for the neural networks.

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:37.790
As a result of this augmented dataset, it
can learn to perform zombie, gorilla movements,

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.959
chicken hopping, even dribbling with a basketball,
you name it.

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:47.379
What’s even more, we can give the AI high-level
commands interactively, and it will try to

00:02:47.379 --> 00:02:51.040
weave the motions together appropriately.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.930
They can also punch each other.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:55.560
Ow.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.680
And all this was learned from a bunch of unorganized
data.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:30.540
What a

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:45.469
time to be alive!

00:03:45.469 --> 00:03:49.480
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

